WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.510
What's the real story behind Aunt Viv being
replaced on The Fresh Prince?

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:08.260
Which cast member on Frasier was the biggest
prima donna?

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:13.126
Behind all the smiles and life lessons, TV
families often have as much drama as real families.

00:00:13.909 --> 00:00:18.200
It wasn't all lovely ladies with golden curls
on the set of The Brady Bunch, especially

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:24.149
when Maureen McCormick and Eve Plumb were
shooting together as sisters Marcia and Jan, respectively.

00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:31.039
"Well, all I hear all day long at school is
how great Marcia is at this, or how wonderful

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:32.039
Marcia did that.

00:00:32.039 --> 00:00:33.039
Marcia, Marcia, Marcia!"

00:00:33.039 --> 00:00:38.219
As McCormick revealed in her 2009 memoir Here's
the Story, Plumb resented her for, quote,

00:00:38.219 --> 00:00:39.829
"getting too much attention."

00:00:39.829 --> 00:00:42.090
And it only became worse as the kids grew
up.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:47.120
Susan Olsen, who played youngest Brady sister
Cindy, told Fox News in 2009 that McCormick

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.920
and Plumb were eventually no longer on speaking
terms, to the point that:

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.879
"Whenever we get together for any project
there will only be one or the other."

00:00:54.879 --> 00:00:56.170
As Olsen further explained,

00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:00.699
"[Eve] got tired of Maureen gaining attention
for herself by regurgitating the tiresome

00:01:00.699 --> 00:01:03.639
and false insinuations that they had a lesbian
affair."

00:01:03.639 --> 00:01:08.240
Fortunately, like most Brady stories, this
one ultimately has a happy ending, as the

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:13.610
onscreen sisters had a chance to settle their
squabble in the HGTV miniseries A Very Brady

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.220
Renovation, which premiered in 2019.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:17.540
As Plumb admitted to E!,

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:22.030
"It had been a really long time since we had
all been together and it's really cool to

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:24.660
see who people are today and what they're
doing.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:25.660
It's awesome…

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:27.840
It's so nice to reconnect with people that
you care about."

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:34.270
Who can forget the public spectacle that was
Charlie Sheen's exit from Two and a Half Men?

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:38.530
It should come as no surprise that his fire
burned a few bridges along the way.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:42.890
The exact timeline of his departure from the
show is unclear, but it undeniably reached

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:48.090
a head when he called creator Chuck Lorre
a "clown" and a "turd" while calling in to

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:49.780
Alex Jones' radio show.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:52.800
Shortly afterwards, Sheen was fired from the
sitcom.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.560
And the punches didn't stop there.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:59.370
After his firing, Sheen went on to tell E!
News that his onscreen brother Jon Cryer was,

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:02.350
quote, "a turncoat, a traitor, [and] a troll."

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:06.740
Cryer apparently hadn't been outspoken enough
in favor of Sheen after his firing, and Sheen

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.909
also claimed that he hadn't reached out to
him, although Cryer claimed otherwise.

00:02:10.909 --> 00:02:16.069
But it seems that there are no hard feelings
too tough to forgive among TV families, as

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:20.219
Lorre later told Entertainment Tonight that
he didn't feel like he and Sheen were ever

00:02:20.219 --> 00:02:21.219
in a feud.

00:02:21.219 --> 00:02:23.150
"I felt like he was my friend.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:25.040
I really loved the guy."

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.459
Cryer, meanwhile, had more mixed feelings
about his time with Sheen.

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:30.829
As he put it in his memoir, So That Happened,

00:02:30.829 --> 00:02:35.450
"To some [...] [Sheen] was a rebel, a truth
teller willing to poke his masters in the eye.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:37.300
[...] Of course, Charlie wasn’t those things.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.629
He was simply lashing out at the people who
told him the party was over."

00:02:41.931 --> 00:02:46.120
The WB series Charmed was anything but, at
least not behind the scenes.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:52.749
If anything, this late 90s/early 2000s drama
about three magical sisters was rather cursed.

00:02:52.749 --> 00:02:57.999
Shannen Doherty and Alyssa Milano played two
of the sisters, and they grew to have a real-life

00:02:57.999 --> 00:03:01.959
beef so big that it led to Doherty's departure
from the show.

00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:04.590
As Milano recounted to E! News in 2020,

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:08.560
"There were times when I'd come in and say,
'Good morning, Shannen,' and she didn't say

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:09.819
anything to me.

00:03:09.819 --> 00:03:12.859
And there were times when she'd come in and
say, 'Good morning, Alyssa,' and I wouldn't

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:14.620
say anything to her."

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:18.120
For Doherty, the routine silent treatments
weren't worth the paycheck.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:19.980
As she told Entertainment Tonight in 2001,

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:23.969
"I'm 30 years old, and I don't have time for
drama in my life anymore."

00:03:23.969 --> 00:03:28.370
With any family infighting, time passes, people
grow, there are regrets, and hopefully there

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:29.750
are amends.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.590
As Milano later admitted to Entertainment
Tonight in 2021,

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:35.319
"I can take responsibility for a lot of our
tension."

00:03:35.319 --> 00:03:38.610
Likewise, Doherty is letting bygones be bygones.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:42.220
As she put it on a 2015 appearance on Sway
in the Morning,

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:43.620
"Are there times that were rough?

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:47.450
Yeah, I mean, I think that when you work that
closely together, there's always going to

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:48.900
be times that are rough."

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:52.629
"Sometimes you don't always get along with
a person 24/7 that you work with."

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:58.239
While Steve Urkel technically had no blood
relation to the Winslow family on Family Matters,

00:03:58.239 --> 00:04:02.420
he was at their house so often that he deserves
an honorary spot at the table.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:07.099
And the irate responses to him from the Winslows
on the show may not have been too far from

00:04:07.099 --> 00:04:08.799
what was going on behind the scenes.

00:04:08.799 --> 00:04:15.470
On a 2021 episode of TVOne's Uncensored, Urkel
actor Jaleel White rehashed some of the early

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:18.950
tension between himself and the older actors
on the show.

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:19.950
As he put it,

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.890
"I was not welcomed to the cast at all.

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:23.140
They know what it was.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.950
I didn’t think anything of it being cast
to be on Family Matters, because it was supposed

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:29.700
to only be a guest spot, one and done."

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:33.830
As the story goes, the other actors bristled
when the network shifted much of the show's

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:35.670
focus over to Urkel.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:39.800
As reported by Entertainment Weekly, there
was one particularly notable occasion that

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:45.080
also involved Reginald VelJohnson and Jo Marie
Payton, who played the Winslow parents.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:49.620
White had to put on a dress, and the two older
actors pressured him against wearing it.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:50.620
As White recalled,

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:53.660
"[They] were very sensitive to putting Black
men in dresses."

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.860
But over time, the familiarity among the cast
improved.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:57.860
According to White,

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:04.001
"[VelJohnson and Payton] do deserve credit
at times for having helped foster a more harmonious environment.

00:05:04.001 --> 00:05:06.300
[...] And we did become very much a family."

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:10.347
Isn't that the best any family, real or fictitious,
can hope for?

00:05:10.654 --> 00:05:15.500
On The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air, Aunt Vivian
may have had the run of the mansion, but behind

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:18.500
the scenes Will Smith was definitely the top
dog.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:23.320
Viewers may recall the jarring recasting of
Aunt Viv, originally played by Janet Hubert

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:27.460
, who was replaced by Daphne Maxwell Reid
in the fourth season.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:30.370
And it wasn't exactly a smooth transition
for the rest of the cast.

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:36.311
In a 2020 interview on WABC's Here &amp; Now,
Hubert discussed her departure from Fresh Prince.

00:05:36.311 --> 00:05:40.090
She explained that she wasn't fired, but rather
that she rejected her contract renewal in

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:42.930
the hopes that the network would return with
a better offer.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:46.460
Instead, they replaced her, though she wasn't
surprised.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:49.330
In her eyes, the production team seemed to
have it out for her.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:50.330
As she put it,

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:53.840
"They told me I had to go into my dressing
room in between takes because I wasn't laughing

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.190
at Will's jokes."

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:56.880
She even went so far as to claim,

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.050
"The cast was offered money to take me down."

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:04.500
Hubert had a chance to confront Smith on a
Fresh Prince reunion that aired on HBO Max

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:05.500
in 2020.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:06.750
She didn't hold back.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:08.430
"I had been banished.

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:14.090
And they said it was you who banished me."

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:15.480
He then offered an apology.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:19.900
He reflected further on the experience during
a Red Table Talk interview, as he admitted

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:22.390
that he was "shaking" during their conversation.

00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:30.910
"That's not who I want to be, and it's not
how I want to make people feel."

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:34.330
Hopefully, Smith makes the most of this second
chance.

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:39.470
The Jackson brothers on the NBC sitcom were
known for their bickering, just like any siblings

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:41.250
who ultimately loved each other.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:45.420
But behind the scenes, it was a little more
violent, as Todd Bridges reportedly slapped

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:49.110
Gary Coleman, although Coleman reportedly
cast the first palm.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:53.240
Bridges, who played older brother Willis,
recounted the story of this backstage fight

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:56.340
in his 2010 autobiography Killing Willis.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:00.180
Bridges felt that Coleman was getting too
egotistical as his character Arnold was gaining

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:02.140
popularity among the fans.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:05.780
According to Bridges, Coleman would always
have an obnoxious comeback to everything that

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:07.510
was said around the set.

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:12.766
On one occasion when he couldn't come up with
a comeback, he slapped Bridges, who then slapped him back.

00:07:12.766 --> 00:07:18.190
Unlike some of the feuds on this list, this
one sadly does not have the chance for a reconciliation.

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:22.010
Each of the three young leads— Bridges,
Coleman, and Dana Plato — struggled with

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:24.260
their fame after the show's conclusion.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:29.550
Plato died of an overdose in 1999, while Coleman
passed away in 2010 after enduring financial

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:32.040
struggles that led to him declaring bankruptcy.

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:36.410
Bridges, who dealt with his own drug addiction,
is now the only surviving member of the original

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:37.720
main cast.

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:38.820
He's doing better, though.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:42.460
As he put it during a 2016 appearance on The
Dr. Oz Show,

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:43.870
"I love life now.

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:45.500
Life is the best thing in the world."

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:51.550
It's hard to forget Roseanne Barr's explosive
exit from the revival of her eponymous ABC

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:53.830
sitcom in 2018.

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:57.840
But just in case you forgot, here's a quick
refresher: Barr tweeted about former Obama

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.620
Administration advisor Valerie Jarrett, in
which she linked her to the Muslim Brotherhood

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:04.580
and Planet of the Apes.

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:07.500
She was then subsequently fired by ABC for
the remark.

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:12.060
The rest of the cast narrowly avoided a complete
cancellation, when Roseanne was rebranded

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:14.790
as the spinoff series The Conners.

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:19.260
In response to Barr's tweet, Sara Gilbert,
who plays Roseanne's daughter Darlene, tweeted

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:20.260
in response,

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:25.230
"Roseanne’s recent comments about Valerie
Jarrett, and so much more, are abhorrent and

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:29.450
do not reflect the beliefs of our cast and
crew or anyone associated with our show.

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:32.620
I am disappointed in her actions to say the
least."

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:36.870
Barr responded in turn, as she claimed that
Gilbert, quote, "destroyed the show and my

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:37.960
life with that tweet."

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:42.830
Despite this rocky episode, The Conners has
gone on to flourish on ABC.

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:47.560
And ultimately it would seem that just like
in real life, some family disputes are irreconcilable.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:53.180
For the most part, the cast of Frasier appears
to have been entirely amicable with each other.

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:54.920
The human cast, that is.

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:59.750
As for family pup Eddie, he was actually portrayed
by two different canine actors.

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:03.779
He was originally played by a Jack Russell
Terrier named Moose, but he came to share

00:09:03.779 --> 00:09:07.010
the role with his son Enzo as he got older.

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:10.600
Often enough, family and business just don't
mix, and that was certainly true on the set

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:11.600
of Frasier.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:15.960
In an interview on the show Animal Magnetism,
Enzo's trainer Mathilde DeCagny revealed,

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:21.370
"Moose and Enzo's relationship is so bad that
they can't even be in the same room together."

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:26.250
Moose may be more to blame, as DeCagny explained
that before he was an actor, his owners gave

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:27.250
him up.

00:09:27.250 --> 00:09:29.540
"He had been nothing else but a terror."

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:33.950
The interviewers then connected the bristling
relationship between Moose and Enzo to the

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:38.550
equally difficult one between human father-son
duo Frasier and Martin Crane.

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:41.400
Sadly, Enzo and Moose have both since passed.

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:45.510
Now that their on-set days are over, hopefully
they've reconciled in doggy heaven.

00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:51.660
It's easy to over-romanticize an iconic classic
sitcom like The Andy Griffith Show.

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:55.730
And certainly, Griffith himself surely wanted
audiences to have that feeling.

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:58.900
As he put it in a 1996 Today Show interview,

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:01.200
"It was of a time gone by."

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:05.610
But despite the simple times in front of the
camera, there was drama behind the scenes.

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:09.150
For the most part, the set of Mayberry seems
to have been as blissful as the show made

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:10.210
it seem.

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:14.100
But one bit of trouble in paradise stemmed
from the difficult relationship between Griffith

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:17.920
and his castmate Frances Bavier, who played
Aunt Bee.

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:22.130
Ron Howard, who played Andy's son Opie, discussed
his memories of making the show during an

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:24.180
interview with the Television Academy.

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:25.180
As he put it,

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:29.360
"I don't think she felt a part of what these
boys were up to and their shenanigans."

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:33.750
While the cast has managed to keep relatively
tight-lipped about any unpleasant feelings,

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:37.760
Griffith did reveal some details about his
relationship with Bavier during an interview

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:40.980
with Ralph Emery on the show On the Record.

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:41.980
As he recalled,

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:44.990
"She wasn't always in real great shape, emotionally."

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:48.680
But toward the end of Bavier's life, Griffith
called her and made amends.

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:49.680
As he recounted,

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:51.410
"I hadn't talked to her in a long time.

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:53.870
[...] She said, 'I'm sorry we didn't get along
better.'

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:55.240
She said, 'It was my fault.'"

00:10:55.240 --> 00:11:00.000
Hopefully, this clearing of the air gave Bavier
some degree of peace.

